--TEST--
`self`, `static` and `parent` are whole types, so a `::` after one in type position is refused naming the owner's own spelling, while the members they cannot reach stay reachable under the class's written name — `rule:types/grammar`, `rule:types/class-scoped-alias`
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Limits {
    public const int MAX = 7;
    type Meta = {n: int};

    // `rule:types/grammar`: the three class keywords are complete atoms, so
    // the `::` is refused where it is written instead of cascading off the
    // token after the type. This is the spelling the type test's own table
    // does not offer (`rule:types/type-test`).
    public static function tests(mixed $v): bool {
        return $v is self::MAX;
    }

    // Every position the type grammar reaches answers the same way, and the
    // refusal recovers as `mixed` so nothing downstream reports a type the
    // program never wrote — one error per spelling, not three.
    public static function takes(self::Meta $m): self::Meta {
        return $m;
    }

    // The two spellings that do work, which is what the refusal's help names:
    // the owner's own name from anywhere, and an alias's bare name inside the
    // body that declares it (`rule:types/class-scoped-alias`).
    public static function fine(Limits::MAX $n, Meta $m): Limits::Meta {
        return $m;
    }

    // The keywords alone are untouched, in every one of their own positions.
    public function me(): self {
        return $this;
    }
}

class Wanting {
    // `static::` and `parent::` are the same refusal. `parent` here has no
    // parent class to name, and the `mixed` recovery is what keeps that from
    // becoming a second error about a type nobody wrote.
    public function f(mixed $v): bool {
        return $v is static::Nope || $v is parent::Nope;
    }
}
--EXPECT--

